1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,328
What do you do when the network never answers?

2
00:00:02,378 --> 00:00:04,968
ecom has asked the card zone to charge a card.

3
00:00:05,018 --> 00:00:09,192
The processing centre is slow, or the reply is lost on the way.

4
00:00:09,242 --> 00:00:11,808
The bank's chosen waiting time runs out.

5
00:00:11,858 --> 00:00:14,136
The buyer's card may already be charged.

6
00:00:14,186 --> 00:00:16,008
Or it may not.

7
00:00:16,058 --> 00:00:18,240
There are three tempting mistakes.

8
00:00:18,290 --> 00:00:22,968
Call it declined, and the buyer pays again, so the card is charged twice.

9
00:00:23,018 --> 00:00:26,280
Send the charge again, and the result is the same.

10
00:00:26,330 --> 00:00:31,416
Wait in the app's memory, and the app restarts, and nobody ever finds out.

11
00:00:31,466 --> 00:00:33,288
Think of a parcel you sent.

12
00:00:33,338 --> 00:00:36,264
It left, but the tracking page says nothing.

13
00:00:36,314 --> 00:00:38,184
You do not post a second parcel.

14
00:00:38,234 --> 00:00:42,984
You check the tracking, and each time you wait a little longer before checking again.

15
00:00:43,034 --> 00:00:47,400
And before the post office closes, you go to the counter and ask a person.

16
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:49,704
A silent charge works the same way.

17
00:00:49,754 --> 00:00:56,664
First, an unknown charge is never declined, and never sent again, because the money may already have moved.

18
00:00:56,714 --> 00:00:59,520
The buyer's page tells them not to pay again.

19
00:00:59,570 --> 00:01:02,064
Then a durable workflow takes over.

20
00:01:02,114 --> 00:01:06,384
It is a task that remembers where it is, even when the app restarts.

21
00:01:06,434 --> 00:01:11,904
There is exactly one for each payment, so two copies can never chase the same charge.

22
00:01:11,954 --> 00:01:17,040
It asks the processing centre about this one charge only, by the payment's own id.

23
00:01:17,090 --> 00:01:18,912
These are questions only.

24
00:01:18,962 --> 00:01:20,784
They never move money.

25
00:01:20,834 --> 00:01:25,752
Between questions it waits, and each wait is longer than the one before.

26
00:01:25,802 --> 00:01:27,624
That pattern is backoff.

27
00:01:27,674 --> 00:01:30,504
It gives a struggling network room to recover.

28
00:01:30,554 --> 00:01:32,376
Found means paid.

29
00:01:32,426 --> 00:01:36,360
Not found means declined, but only once the network has had time to know.

30
00:01:36,410 --> 00:01:41,568
If the charge is found after the buyer's page has closed, the order is already over.

31
00:01:41,618 --> 00:01:43,512
So the money goes back to the card.

32
00:01:43,562 --> 00:01:48,864
The questions stop before the processing centre's daily cut-off, less the bank's chosen margin.

33
00:01:48,914 --> 00:01:51,432
That leaves time to give money back the same day.

34
00:01:51,482 --> 00:01:54,624
Anything still unknown then goes to manual review.

35
00:01:54,674 --> 00:01:58,704
That means a person at the bank checks with the network, and records the answer.

36
00:01:58,754 --> 00:02:00,576
Now watch the product.

37
00:02:00,626 --> 00:02:06,480
On the buyer's side, the page says we're confirming your payment, and adds: don't pay again.

38
00:02:06,530 --> 00:02:10,560
In the merchant portal, the payment's status reads checking with the bank.

39
00:02:10,610 --> 00:02:15,312
Open it, and the calls to the card scheme show one debit line, with no answer.

40
00:02:15,362 --> 00:02:18,336
A little later, the workflow gets its answer.

41
00:02:18,386 --> 00:02:23,184
The status changes to paid, or to declined, and the merchant is told.

42
00:02:23,234 --> 00:02:25,056
Now the rare case.

43
00:02:25,106 --> 00:02:28,272
The day's deadline nears, and there is still no answer.

44
00:02:28,322 --> 00:02:30,888
The status changes to being checked by a person.

45
00:02:30,938 --> 00:02:36,624
In the bank's admin portal, the payment appears under needs review, with how long it has waited.

46
00:02:36,674 --> 00:02:40,440
An admin asks the network, then picks debited or not debited.

47
00:02:40,490 --> 00:02:44,712
For debited, the admin enters the network's reference for the charge.

48
00:02:44,762 --> 00:02:49,752
Either way, the admin writes a note: who at the network confirmed it, and how.

49
00:02:49,802 --> 00:02:51,984
That note is saved for audit.

50
00:02:52,034 --> 00:02:55,968
The admin presses resolve, and the payment takes its final state.

51
00:02:56,018 --> 00:02:57,840
What does this protect?

52
00:02:57,890 --> 00:03:01,176
It protects the buyer from paying twice for one order.

53
00:03:01,226 --> 00:03:06,768
It protects the merchant from shipping goods that were never paid, or refusing an order that was.

54
00:03:06,818 --> 00:03:11,184
It protects the bank from a charge nobody can explain at the end of the day.

55
00:03:11,234 --> 00:03:14,688
And it does not depend on the app staying up, or on anyone remembering.

56
00:03:14,738 --> 00:03:16,560
So back to the parcel.

57
00:03:16,610 --> 00:03:23,544
Keep checking, wait longer each time, remember across restarts, and ask a person before the office closes.

58
00:03:23,594 --> 00:03:26,160
Next: how the shop hears about a payment.
